1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Your viewing history can say something
about how you remember.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,500
But it should never become
another surveillance profile.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,000
WatchTree lets you build a private memory tree —
from links you choose, not data taken from you.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
WatchTree never reads your YouTube account.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,500
There is no OAuth, no API key,
no automatic history import.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,500
You paste a public YouTube URL.

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,000
WatchTree validates it and stores
only what you deliberately add.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You can attach a watched date, mark a rewatch,
or leave a private note.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Every record enters your collection
only because you chose it.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
As your collection grows,
WatchTree builds your private tree.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
It shows your event count,
repeat tendency, and time rhythm.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,500
These signals come only
from records you provided.

13
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,500
User-added labels are shown as unverified.

14
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:03,000
WatchTree does not generate an AI profile.
It reflects what you chose to remember.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,500
WatchTree compares your records against
clearly synthetic viewing archetypes —

16
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:11,000
not real people.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,500
Matching is deterministic and explainable.

18
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,500
Each candidate shows bounded evidence
drawn from your own records.

19
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:24,000
When signal is insufficient,
WatchTree says so.

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
There is no soulmate score.
There is no real-person scan.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,500
Before any result is revealed,
you select which evidence to share

22
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,500
and give explicit consent.

23
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:41,500
The mutual state shown here is simulated —
it does not represent another real user.

24
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:48,000
Your consent choices persist across reloads.

25
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Privacy is a product feature,
not a footer promise.

26
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
You can turn matching off,
exclude specific records,

27
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
or delete your entire collection.

28
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
After deletion, WatchTree restores
a clean empty state.

29
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,500
Your data lifecycle is always in your hands.

30
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Base44 is not only where WatchTree is hosted.

31
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,500
It is the authenticated data, permission,
function, and deployment layer

32
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:19,500
behind the entire privacy model.

33
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:22,500
Base44 Auth verifies every caller.

34
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:28,000
Owner-scoped Entities enforce row-level security
through created_by_id.

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Caller-authenticated Deno Functions
protect server state.

36
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Base44 hosting deploys the verified release.

37
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
WatchTree by Resonance.
Built for the Base44 Dev Build-Off.
